WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.920
Hello everyone, today here I've got a stack of&nbsp;
physics books that I got out from the library,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.920 --> 00:00:11.760
and they all deal with ideas relating to time&nbsp;
or space and time travel. I thought it would be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.760 --> 00:00:17.340
really interesting to see how these books go about&nbsp;
describing and illustrating time travel because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.340 --> 00:00:23.820
time travel is something that usually belongs&nbsp;
in fiction books or sci-fi and is treated in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.820 --> 00:00:30.240
very fanciful way but these are non-fiction books&nbsp;
and some are written by quite serious scientists,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.240 --> 00:00:34.080
so I thought it would be interesting&nbsp;
to see how they unravel the same ideas,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.080 --> 00:00:40.020
and how time travel is treated by&nbsp;
people who are dedicated to studying it.

00:00:40.020 --> 00:00:46.140
The first book in our lineup today is Geometry,&nbsp;
Relativity and the Fourth Dimension by Rudy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.140 --> 00:00:50.880
Rucker. Now if you've been following my short&nbsp;
videos about the fourth dimension you'll&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.880 --> 00:00:56.280
have met some ideas from this book already but&nbsp;
tucked away in its chapters there's a compelling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.280 --> 00:01:01.980
discussion on time travel. This diagram here&nbsp;
is to get us thinking in terms of space-time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.980 --> 00:01:09.120
it shows these flat slices of our world that&nbsp;
are moving forward in time. In this particular&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.120 --> 00:01:16.680
example there are two characters; a square and&nbsp;
a triangle, living on a flat plane. This is what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.680 --> 00:01:24.300
their world looks like at 12 o'clock but five&nbsp;
minutes later at the slice 12:05 the triangle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.300 --> 00:01:32.580
has moved towards the square maybe to say hello&nbsp;
and then five minutes later again at 12:10 the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.580 --> 00:01:39.660
triangle has moved away meaning that in space-time&nbsp;
you could draw the triangle as a worm that snakes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.660 --> 00:01:47.520
over here and then back again whereas Mr. Square&nbsp;
remains stationary the entire time and their world&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.520 --> 00:01:53.520
line is just this straight line. This just shows&nbsp;
two characters but you could try to represent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.520 --> 00:01:59.940
the entire world or Universe using these world&nbsp;
lines and it would be a vast tangle of worms.

00:01:59.940 --> 00:02:06.000
You could represent every atom by one line&nbsp;
and because every atom in a person's body is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:12.660
replaced every seven years or so there wouldn't&nbsp;
actually be a single thread that goes the whole&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.660 --> 00:02:19.800
length of a person's life. A living individual is&nbsp;
a persistent pattern of world lines rather than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.800 --> 00:02:25.920
a particular collection of particles. Drawing&nbsp;
world lines helps you think of space-time as a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.920 --> 00:02:31.800
structure all of its own where the universe&nbsp;
at any instant is a 3D cross-section but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.800 --> 00:02:37.320
that does make us wonder why we can't see&nbsp;
the past or future if they really exist.

00:02:37.320 --> 00:02:42.960
One idea is that maybe all instances of&nbsp;
time do exist but because our memories&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.960 --> 00:02:47.940
only relate to events that happened at&nbsp;
previous time steps to our present moment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.940 --> 00:02:52.500
we get the illusion that we are&nbsp;
moving forwards in time always.

00:02:52.500 --> 00:02:58.080
The author then makes a slight departure&nbsp;
from physics but this next diagram helps&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.080 --> 00:03:04.800
me to understand meditation better than any yoga&nbsp;
class I've been to. He asks if there is a way to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.800 --> 00:03:11.220
be conscious in a static space-time instead&nbsp;
of always moving forwards. Well one trick to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.220 --> 00:03:17.760
achieve the feeling of timelessness is to stop&nbsp;
thinking new thoughts because without that it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.760 --> 00:03:22.920
hard to distinguish one moment from the next.&nbsp;
To achieve this timelessness you could either&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.920 --> 00:03:28.620
think nothing at all which is not easy or&nbsp;
concentrate your attention on a repetitive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.620 --> 00:03:34.320
thought loop like a mantra. Thinking nothing,&nbsp;
you can't differentiate one instant from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.320 --> 00:03:40.080
next and doing a mantra you can't differentiate&nbsp;
one repetition of the mantra from the next.

00:03:40.080 --> 00:03:46.020
This next diagram here shows a way to time&nbsp;
travel to the past although it only works&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.020 --> 00:03:52.380
in a universe where time is circular. In this&nbsp;
universe the time dimension is wrapped into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.380 --> 00:03:59.820
a cylinder and space extends infinitely out to&nbsp;
the sides. The circle here represents the world&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.820 --> 00:04:05.580
line of the earth. If we say that it takes&nbsp;
one trillion years for time to repeat itself,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.580 --> 00:04:12.420
so that is the circumference of this world line,&nbsp;
to time travel we could leave the Earth speeding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.420 --> 00:04:18.180
off to the side for say a quarter of a trillion&nbsp;
years then we could turn around and travel back&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.180 --> 00:04:24.240
this way for say half a trillion years then&nbsp;
turn around again and take another quarter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.240 --> 00:04:29.160
of a trillion years to get back to where&nbsp;
we started but because time is circular&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.160 --> 00:04:36.060
we will intersect the world line of the Earth&nbsp;
in its past. Ideas of circular time have been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.060 --> 00:04:41.820
proposed in physics but there are plenty of&nbsp;
practical reasons why a journey like this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.820 --> 00:04:47.520
taking a trillion years and needing to travel&nbsp;
close to the speed of light is not going to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.520 --> 00:04:53.700
work. You'd need a rocket the size of a galaxy&nbsp;
to have enough fuel and you'd probably get lost.

00:04:53.700 --> 00:04:59.760
This next book is Paul Davies: About Time. In a&nbsp;
chapter on time reversal there is a discussion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.760 --> 00:05:07.020
about antimatter and how there are things called&nbsp;
positrons which are like anti-electrons. They&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.020 --> 00:05:13.560
have the opposite charge but maybe they also&nbsp;
go back in time. Electrons and positrons are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.560 --> 00:05:19.980
created together in pairs, usually it's from&nbsp;
a photon colliding with an atom. They fly off&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.980 --> 00:05:26.160
and hope to never see each other again because&nbsp;
if a positron were to run into an electron then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.160 --> 00:05:32.040
the pair would instantly annihilate and reverse&nbsp;
this pair creation process giving back photons.

00:05:32.040 --> 00:05:39.960
This is a space-time diagram where here&nbsp;
a photon coming into point A creates an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.960 --> 00:05:46.260
electron-positron pair. The positron then meets&nbsp;
with another electron and annihilates giving&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.260 --> 00:05:52.980
back a photon. At this time T an observer&nbsp;
would see three particles; an electron,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.980 --> 00:05:59.700
a positron and another electron, but according&nbsp;
to Richard Feynman this zigzag track could be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.700 --> 00:06:05.760
viewed as the worldline of just one particle,&nbsp;
an electron that travels back in time between&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.760 --> 00:06:12.000
these annihilation and creation events. That's&nbsp;
why the arrow here for the positron is pointing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.000 --> 00:06:18.180
backwards. Feynman's theory was that this is&nbsp;
all just one electron and it travels back in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.180 --> 00:06:24.300
time for that brief period where it is considered&nbsp;
a positron. This idea could be extended to include&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.300 --> 00:06:30.240
many more electrons and positrons and you could&nbsp;
say that all the electrons in the universe are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.240 --> 00:06:36.840
just the same one particle. That there is just&nbsp;
one electron making up the entire universe. It&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.840 --> 00:06:42.840
offers a nice explanation for why all electrons&nbsp;
appear to be identical but that theory comes with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.840 --> 00:06:48.780
problems of its own. It would also predict that&nbsp;
the universe is made exactly of half matter and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.780 --> 00:06:55.140
half antimatter but we don't see nearly as much&nbsp;
antimatter as we do normal matter and the arrow&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.140 --> 00:07:01.500
of time in our universe appears to point forwards&nbsp;
rather than having a symmetry pointing backwards.

00:07:01.500 --> 00:07:07.080
Another way to time travel to the past is&nbsp;
detailed in this next book; Time Travel in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.080 --> 00:07:12.660
Einstein's Universe by J Richard Gott. Gott&nbsp;
mentions something called cosmic strings,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.660 --> 00:07:19.140
which are thin strands of high-density material&nbsp;
left over from the early Universe. This diagram&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.140 --> 00:07:24.900
is designed to show what space-time looks like&nbsp;
around a cosmic string. It's like a piece of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.900 --> 00:07:31.800
pizza missing a slice. If we take out that slice&nbsp;
and stick it together we get a cone. Here my&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.800 --> 00:07:38.580
pencil is the cosmic string and the cone is what&nbsp;
a horizontal plane of space-time around the string&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.580 --> 00:07:44.400
looks like. Suppose that here's the Earth, here's&nbsp;
the cosmic string and here is a quasar split&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.400 --> 00:07:49.860
across the region of the missing slice. These&nbsp;
straight lines show the path that light would take&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.860 --> 00:07:55.620
from the quasar to reach Earth and here's what&nbsp;
those paths look like in the conical space-time.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.620 --> 00:08:01.740
To us on Earth it will appear as if there are two&nbsp;
different quasars even though it's the same object&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.740 --> 00:08:07.680
and even though the light is traveling on the&nbsp;
shortest straightest path to us from the quasar,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.680 --> 00:08:14.040
one of the paths will be shorter. This is the&nbsp;
principle of gravitational lensing and may be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.040 --> 00:08:20.100
what helps us to find cosmic strings. The light&nbsp;
traveling along path two has beaten the light&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.100 --> 00:08:26.760
traveling along path one and if it was a rocket&nbsp;
traveling along path two, traveling at nearly the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.760 --> 00:08:32.640
speed of light, it could have also beaten light&nbsp;
path one. If cosmic strings exist then you could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.640 --> 00:08:39.000
travel in a spaceship and outrun a light beam&nbsp;
by taking the shorter of two paths around a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.000 --> 00:08:45.060
cosmic string. When the rocket arrives at Earth,&nbsp;
the light showing its departure from the quasar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.060 --> 00:08:52.680
won't have arrived yet so you could look back and&nbsp;
see yourself still on the quasar about to depart.

00:08:52.680 --> 00:08:58.380
On another page in this book there's instructions&nbsp;
on how to build a time machine that can visit the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.380 --> 00:09:03.960
future. To construct this time machine you&nbsp;
need to find the mass of the planet Jupiter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.960 --> 00:09:10.920
and construct it around yourself into a spherical&nbsp;
shell. That will create a huge gravitational well&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.920 --> 00:09:16.920
around you but because it's a sphere it will&nbsp;
perfectly cancel out in every direction and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.920 --> 00:09:22.440
inside the shell you will feel unaffected.&nbsp;
We're just teetering on the limit of mass&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.440 --> 00:09:27.600
concentration that would create a black hole&nbsp;
so you don't want to go overboard with this.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:27.600 --> 00:09:33.960
Just outside of the spherical shell you would&nbsp;
be torn apart by tidal forces generated by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.960 --> 00:09:38.580
its gravitational attraction but inside&nbsp;
the sphere you would be perfectly safe.

00:09:38.580 --> 00:09:46.500
This diagram simplifies the situation down to&nbsp;
just a two-dimensional shell so it's drawn around&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.500 --> 00:09:52.800
this time traveler here as a circle but for us it&nbsp;
would actually be a sphere. If you imagine an ant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.800 --> 00:09:59.460
falling into something this shape it would require&nbsp;
a lot of energy to crawl back out. The same thing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.460 --> 00:10:07.320
will happen with our photons. Photons emitted by&nbsp;
us will lose a lot of energy on the way out and be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:07.320 --> 00:10:14.160
redshifted turning into longer wavelength light.&nbsp;
Photons from outside of the well that fall in will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:14.160 --> 00:10:19.860
pick up energy as they fall. The person inside&nbsp;
of the well would see these waves oscillating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:19.860 --> 00:10:25.980
faster than they did on the outside and if the&nbsp;
people outside the well had been using a clock&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:25.980 --> 00:10:31.260
which relied on the oscillation of these waves,&nbsp;
the person inside of the well would see these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:36.968 --> 00:10:42.368
disassemble the well he would step out of his&nbsp;
time machine and be younger than those around him.

00:10:42.368 --> 00:10:48.968
Another way to time travel to the future is&nbsp;
by moving really fast. Usually running away&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:48.968 --> 00:10:54.128
on a rocket going near the speed of light,&nbsp;
leaving Earth for a bit then turning around&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:54.128 --> 00:10:59.948
and coming back and being younger than everyone&nbsp;
around you. I think this diagram does a good job&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:59.948 --> 00:11:07.208
of explaining why a clock on a rocket ship&nbsp;
moving fast actually ticks more slowly than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:07.208 --> 00:11:12.188
one that's left on Earth. If you construct&nbsp;
a clock that uses the bouncing of light off&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:12.188 --> 00:11:16.808
mirrors to measure the time then this is what&nbsp;
our clock on Earth would look like. The light&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:16.808 --> 00:11:21.968
would predictably bounce between the two mirrors&nbsp;
and the clock would tick every time it hit one.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:21.968 --> 00:11:28.628
Give that same clock to an astronaut on a moving&nbsp;
rocket and if we observed that astronaut from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:28.628 --> 00:11:33.788
outside of their space station this is what we&nbsp;
would see. The mirrors are still separated by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:33.788 --> 00:11:39.788
the same distance but because the rocket is&nbsp;
moving past us we will see the light bounce&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:39.788 --> 00:11:45.308
off the bottom mirror and then bounce off the top&nbsp;
mirror but the distance between the mirrors will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:45.308 --> 00:11:50.828
no longer be the initial three feet separation.&nbsp;
It will be longer because it's traveled on this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:50.828 --> 00:11:56.408
diagonal line. Because light travels at a&nbsp;
constant speed, if it's traveling a greater&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:56.408 --> 00:12:01.748
distance then there'll be a greater time delay&nbsp;
between these two ticks of the clock. So this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:01.748 --> 00:12:07.448
astronauts clock will tick more slowly compared&nbsp;
to ours. It will happen to everything on board.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:07.448 --> 00:12:13.808
Even the astronaut's heart is a kind of clock and&nbsp;
that is why when the astronaut returns to earth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:13.808 --> 00:12:19.268
after their journey they won't have physically&nbsp;
aged as much as someone who was left on Earth.

00:12:21.188 --> 00:12:26.948
Most books that mention time travel will have&nbsp;
one of these, a Minkowski or a space-time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:26.948 --> 00:12:31.928
diagram. These diagonal lines represent&nbsp;
an object moving at the speed of light.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:31.928 --> 00:12:39.068
Anything less steep than that will be inside&nbsp;
of these cones and will be from either the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:39.068 --> 00:12:45.968
past or the future of Point P. Because things&nbsp;
can travel from anywhere in this region to P,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:45.968 --> 00:12:52.388
anything in this cone could have affected P, it&nbsp;
is its past light cone. Same with the future,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:52.388 --> 00:12:58.688
anything from P could affect anything in&nbsp;
this region. But these points A and B are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:58.688 --> 00:13:04.868
outside of the light cones, this is the region&nbsp;
of Elsewhere. Whilst A and B might happen at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:04.868 --> 00:13:10.928
same time as P they cannot affect P because&nbsp;
light could not travel from here to here.

00:13:10.928 --> 00:13:16.268
All observers agree on the light paths&nbsp;
because light travels at the same speed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:16.268 --> 00:13:21.908
for all observers but depending on how&nbsp;
fast you are moving yourself the axes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:21.908 --> 00:13:26.408
x and t will actually move and&nbsp;
change based on your reference frame.

00:13:26.408 --> 00:13:32.138
And now here are some highly commended&nbsp;
extra entries. Space-time breaking up,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:32.138 --> 00:13:38.408
where at a mini-microscopic level quantum effects&nbsp;
are so violent that they start to tear space and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:38.408 --> 00:13:44.408
time apart completely creating a sponge-like&nbsp;
structure of wormholes and bridges. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:44.408 --> 00:13:50.648
activity would be unnoticed by even a subatomic&nbsp;
particle which would be larger than the wormhole.

00:13:50.648 --> 00:13:56.108
This one shows a rocket stuck inside&nbsp;
of a black hole. The rocket is always&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:56.108 --> 00:14:00.188
dragged back down and so are light&nbsp;
signals that they try to send out,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:00.188 --> 00:14:04.508
there's no escape and no way to&nbsp;
let anyone know that you are here.

00:14:04.508 --> 00:14:11.828
This figure shows black and white holes.&nbsp;
A is a collapse to a black hole and B is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:11.828 --> 00:14:16.448
an explosion from a white hole. This one's&nbsp;
too complicated for me to understand enough&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:16.448 --> 00:14:20.228
to explain but the study of black&nbsp;
and white holes looks very cool.

00:14:20.228 --> 00:14:27.968
Here's an interesting model called a self-creating&nbsp;
universe where the universe has a time loop at its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:27.968 --> 00:14:34.208
beginning meaning the question of what happened&nbsp;
before the universe existed doesn't really apply&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:34.208 --> 00:14:40.568
because according to this the universe is its&nbsp;
own creation and if you were to go back in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:40.568 --> 00:14:45.428
time throughout the history of the universe you&nbsp;
would end up intersecting with your own tail.

00:14:47.168 --> 00:14:50.288
Thanks for joining me on this&nbsp;
look through some physics books.&nbsp;

00:14:50.288 --> 00:14:54.248
Thanks to my patrons for making this&nbsp;
video possible and a special shout&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:54.248 --> 00:14:57.368
out to today's Patron Cat of the Day Freeway.

